<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apamea, Syria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Apamea on the Orontes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apamea was an ancient city on the Orontes river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, founded around 300 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> and named after his wife Apama. It was the military base of the Seleucid kingdom, where its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> were kept, and later a large Roman city with a colonnaded street nearly 2 km long. Its ruins, near modern Qalaat al-Madiq, were badly looted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apamea-syria.jpg" alt="Apamea, Syria" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apamea_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Apamea stands on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes River</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, on the edge of the modern town of Qalaat al-Madiq, about 55 km north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> and overlooking the Ghab valley. The site was enclosed in a loop of the Orontes which, with a lake and marshes, gave it a peninsular form, hence its other name Cherronêsos. It lay at a strategic crossroads for eastern commerce and became one of the four cities of the Syrian tetrapolis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city began as Pella, a Macedonian military settlement founded after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-diadochi">Wars of the Diadochi</a>. Seleucus I Nicator gave it the status of a polis around 300 BCE, fortified it and named it after his Bactrian wife Apama, daughter of the Sogdian warlord Spitamenes. Seleucus made it a military base with 500 elephants and a stud of 30,000 mares and 300 stallions. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> under Quintus Aemilius Secundus in 6 CE counted 117,000 citizens. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> razed its fortress in 64 BCE when the city was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and it held out against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> for three years in the revolt under Quintus Caecilius Bassus, until Cassius arrived in 46 BCE.</p><p>Much of the city was destroyed in the earthquake of 115 CE and rebuilt through the second century. From 218 to 234 the legion II Parthica was stationed there. In the early fifth century Apamea became capital of the province of Syria Secunda. Khosrow I destroyed it in the sixth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz">Shahrbaraz</a> took it in 613 during the Byzantine–Sasanian War. After the Muslim conquest it was partly resettled as Afāmiya, regained importance under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanid dynasty</a> and was taken by Tancred after the murder of its ruler Khalaf ibn Mula'ib. An earthquake destroyed it in 1152.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Great Colonnade, the city's main avenue running nearly 2 km, was among the longest in the Roman world. Rebuilt after 115 CE with columns 9 metres high, it connected the baths, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a>, the nymphaeum and other buildings, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> later restored parts of it. The Roman theatre, built in the early Seleucid period and remodelled under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and Hadrian, held an estimated 20,000 or more and is among the largest surviving in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>; only one-eighth of it has been exposed.</p><p>A synagogue of about 391 CE with a mosaic floor bearing 19 donor inscriptions was replaced in the fifth century by a Christian church. The Great Hunting Mosaic of 415–420 CE, found in 1935 in what was probably the governor's palace, and a reconstructed section of the colonnade are in the Art &amp; History Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. The site has suffered damage from looting since the onset of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrian-civil-war">Syrian civil war</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apamea, Syria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Niya lay in the middle Orontes valley, probably near <b>Apamea</b>, in a region of marshes and fertile land.</blockquote><a href="/state/niya">Niya</a></li></ul></section>
